WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.438
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.438 --> 00:00:06.376
learn to connect button actions to model state,

00:00:06.376 --> 00:00:09.315
refresh visible feedback, and check pointer and

00:00:09.315 --> 00:00:10.440
keyboard behavior.

00:00:11.102 --> 00:00:13.713
These skills help applications respond

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.873
consistently to user input while keeping their

00:00:16.873 --> 00:00:19.552
data and visible controls in agreement.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.297
A campus workshop desk needs a booking counter.

00:00:23.297 --> 00:00:26.206
Each successful activation should reserve one

00:00:26.206 --> 00:00:27.240
available place.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.083
We'll begin with four places, then adapt the

00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:32.668
same behavior to the course's three-seat

00:00:32.668 --> 00:00:33.185
example.

00:00:33.849 --> 00:00:36.708
We'll inspect the real window, use its button

00:00:36.708 --> 00:00:39.566
with both pointer and keyboard input, and try

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:41.281
another activation at zero.

00:00:41.281 --> 00:00:43.949
The label should follow the model, and the

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:46.998
button should stop offering reservations when no

00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:47.824
seats remain.

00:00:48.516 --> 00:00:51.557
In the Workspace, we'll use Main.java and the

00:00:51.557 --> 00:00:53.855
supplied standalone JavaFX runner.

00:00:53.855 --> 00:00:56.828
The notebook uses the same kind of interface

00:00:56.828 --> 00:00:59.801
instructions inside its supplied Fx support.

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:02.977
Here, closing the last window lets the separate

00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:03.991
program finish.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.349
First, separate the booking rule from its

00:01:28.349 --> 00:01:28.835
display.

00:01:28.835 --> 00:01:31.689
SeatCounter will be our model: an ordinary Java

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:33.935
object that owns the remaining count.

00:01:33.935 --> 00:01:36.667
Private state keeps other code from assigning

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.821
the count directly.

00:01:37.821 --> 00:01:40.735
A getter lets the interface read it, and reserve

00:01:40.735 --> 00:01:42.435
provides the allowed update.

00:02:05.363 --> 00:02:08.080
The constructor stores the starting number.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.735
The reserve method subtracts one only when

00:02:10.735 --> 00:02:12.694
remaining is greater than zero.

00:02:12.694 --> 00:02:15.601
That condition protects the model even if some

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:18.508
caller invokes reserve without going through a

00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:18.950
button.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.857
The model contains no Label or other interface

00:02:21.857 --> 00:02:22.363
control.

00:02:22.402 --> 00:02:25.160
The supplied Application runner gives us the

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.354
start method and its primary Stage.

00:02:27.354 --> 00:02:30.050
Inside it, we'll create the model with four

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.871
places, a Label that reads its initial value,

00:02:32.871 --> 00:02:34.877
and a Button labeled Book place.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:09.218
These controls form the view: what the user sees

00:03:09.218 --> 00:03:10.033
and operates.

00:03:10.033 --> 00:03:12.979
Constructing a button does not reserve a place.

00:03:12.979 --> 00:03:15.424
The initial label reads the model once.

00:03:15.424 --> 00:03:18.057
It will not automatically reread the count

00:03:18.057 --> 00:03:21.066
whenever that count changes, so we must register

00:03:21.066 --> 00:03:22.759
behavior that refreshes it.

00:03:22.817 --> 00:03:25.580
An action event notifies the program that a

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.993
control was activated.

00:03:26.993 --> 00:03:29.691
setOnAction registers a callback, which is

00:03:29.691 --> 00:03:31.747
behavior JavaFX will call later.

00:03:31.747 --> 00:03:34.510
Our lambda receives the event and describes

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.594
three steps: change the model, update the label,

00:03:37.594 --> 00:03:40.292
then update whether the button is enabled.

00:04:01.942 --> 00:04:04.205
The arrow introduces the lambda body.

00:04:04.205 --> 00:04:06.957
Registering this body stores its behavior; it

00:04:06.957 --> 00:04:09.404
does not run these three statements now.

00:04:09.404 --> 00:04:12.278
The model and control references stay the same,

00:04:12.278 --> 00:04:14.908
while the objects they refer to can change.

00:04:14.908 --> 00:04:17.721
This uses the captured-reference rule from our

00:04:17.721 --> 00:04:19.067
earlier lambda lesson.

00:04:19.116 --> 00:04:21.909
When called, reserve changes the model first.

00:04:21.909 --> 00:04:24.702
setText then builds the visible text from the

00:04:24.702 --> 00:04:26.005
updated getter value.

00:04:26.005 --> 00:04:28.860
Finally, setDisable receives whether the count

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:29.605
equals zero.

00:04:29.605 --> 00:04:32.273
True disables this control; false leaves it

00:04:32.273 --> 00:04:32.770
enabled.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.749
Reversing these responsibilities can leave stale

00:04:35.749 --> 00:04:38.666
feedback even when the model changes correctly.

00:04:38.715 --> 00:04:41.454
Arrange the label above the button in a VBox,

00:04:41.454 --> 00:04:44.377
using the spacing and padding from the preceding

00:04:44.377 --> 00:04:44.803
lesson.

00:04:44.803 --> 00:04:47.664
Attach that root to the Scene, set the separate

00:04:47.664 --> 00:04:49.673
window title, and show the Stage.

00:04:49.673 --> 00:04:52.169
The initial console report is outside the

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:54.969
handler, so a later reservation will not print

00:04:54.969 --> 00:04:56.065
that report again.

00:05:24.104 --> 00:05:27.124
Finish start and the supplied main entry point.

00:05:27.124 --> 00:05:29.566
The launch call begins this standalone

00:05:29.566 --> 00:05:30.337
application.

00:05:30.337 --> 00:05:33.100
Its short action handlers run on the JavaFX

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.120
Application Thread, where our interface updates

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:36.570
belong.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.204
We are not adding background work to this

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:39.654
lesson.

00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:51.638
Let's click Run to see the workshop counter.

00:05:51.638 --> 00:05:54.098
Four is the configured starting count.

00:05:54.098 --> 00:05:57.076
Registration should leave it unchanged until a

00:05:57.076 --> 00:05:58.889
person activates the button.

00:06:08.251 --> 00:06:11.289
The window shows Places four above Book place.

00:06:11.289 --> 00:06:14.194
The console's initial report also says four.

00:06:14.194 --> 00:06:17.165
The handler is registered, but no reservation

00:06:17.165 --> 00:06:18.024
has occurred.

00:06:18.024 --> 00:06:20.401
We'll click the visible button once.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.377
The label now shows three.

00:06:25.377 --> 00:06:28.051
That click requested an action; the handler

00:06:28.051 --> 00:06:31.036
changed the model, refreshed the label, and kept

00:06:31.036 --> 00:06:32.217
the button enabled.

00:06:32.217 --> 00:06:34.829
Keyboard focus identifies the control that

00:06:34.829 --> 00:06:36.322
receives keyboard input.

00:06:36.322 --> 00:06:39.307
After this click, Book place has focus, so Space

00:06:39.307 --> 00:06:41.110
can activate the same action.

00:06:43.343 --> 00:06:46.197
Space reaches the same handler and leaves two.

00:06:46.197 --> 00:06:49.174
Focus and enablement answer different questions:

00:06:49.174 --> 00:06:52.152
which control receives the key, and whether that

00:06:52.152 --> 00:06:53.765
control permits an action.

00:06:53.765 --> 00:06:56.743
We'll activate twice more to reach the boundary.

00:07:00.926 --> 00:07:03.623
The label reaches zero, and Book place is

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:04.215
disabled.

00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:06.714
Let's try another pointer click on it.

00:07:06.714 --> 00:07:09.411
The visible zero should remain, because a

00:07:09.411 --> 00:07:12.502
disabled button does not dispatch this ordinary

00:07:12.502 --> 00:07:13.226
activation.

00:07:16.475 --> 00:07:17.266
Zero remains.

00:07:17.266 --> 00:07:20.066
The disabled button prevents this user action,

00:07:20.066 --> 00:07:22.927
while the condition inside reserve protects the

00:07:22.927 --> 00:07:25.301
model if it is called directly at zero.

00:07:25.301 --> 00:07:27.188
These are two different checks.

00:07:27.188 --> 00:07:30.049
The visible window proves the control response;

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:32.666
the direct-call rule comes from the model's

00:07:32.666 --> 00:07:33.275
condition.

00:07:33.539 --> 00:07:36.371
We'll close this window before changing the

00:07:36.371 --> 00:07:37.424
starting inputs.

00:07:37.424 --> 00:07:39.927
The standalone process can now finish.

00:07:39.927 --> 00:07:42.890
A complete rerun constructs a fresh model and

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:45.787
fresh controls; it does not continue the old

00:07:45.787 --> 00:07:46.314
counter.

00:07:50.134 --> 00:07:53.120
For the original seat counter, start with three,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.981
use Remaining in the label, Reserve one on the

00:07:55.981 --> 00:07:58.780
button, and Seat Counter as the window title.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:01.454
The model class, handler order, layout, and

00:08:01.454 --> 00:08:03.009
scene size stay the same.

00:08:36.405 --> 00:08:39.143
Before running, predict the initial report and

00:08:39.143 --> 00:08:39.500
label.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:42.118
Then trace one pointer click followed by two

00:08:42.118 --> 00:08:44.499
Space activations on the focused button.

00:08:44.499 --> 00:08:47.356
What should the label and button show afterward?

00:08:47.356 --> 00:08:49.439
What will another click attempt do?

00:08:49.439 --> 00:08:51.760
Pause and record your prediction before

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:52.355
observing.

00:09:00.472 --> 00:09:03.363
Now click Run and compare the actual three-seat

00:09:03.363 --> 00:09:05.147
counter with your prediction.

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:16.394
The window starts at Remaining three.

00:09:16.394 --> 00:09:19.356
No seat was used by registering the callback.

00:09:19.356 --> 00:09:22.186
We'll perform the pointer activation first,

00:09:22.186 --> 00:09:24.884
followed by the two keyboard activations.

00:09:32.391 --> 00:09:35.481
Remaining is zero, and Reserve one is disabled.

00:09:35.481 --> 00:09:38.307
The label has followed the model after each

00:09:38.307 --> 00:09:38.767
action.

00:09:38.767 --> 00:09:41.791
Try the additional click and check whether any

00:09:41.791 --> 00:09:43.566
new reservation is allowed.

00:09:46.867 --> 00:09:48.904
The disabled attempt leaves zero.

00:09:48.904 --> 00:09:51.866
The original console report is still the initial

00:09:51.866 --> 00:09:54.087
three, because it was printed during

00:09:54.087 --> 00:09:56.618
construction, outside the action handler.

00:09:56.618 --> 00:09:59.456
A useful check records both the visible states

00:09:59.456 --> 00:10:02.110
and which part of the program produces each

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:02.542
report.

00:10:02.656 --> 00:10:05.862
Close this window and run the unchanged program

00:10:05.862 --> 00:10:06.544
once more.

00:10:06.544 --> 00:10:09.546
A fresh model should restore three available

00:10:09.546 --> 00:10:11.456
seats and an enabled button.

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:18.301
The source is unchanged.

00:10:18.301 --> 00:10:21.399
Click Run again to check complete recreation.

00:10:30.405 --> 00:10:33.255
The fresh Seat Counter is back at Remaining

00:10:33.255 --> 00:10:35.309
three with Reserve one enabled.

00:10:35.309 --> 00:10:38.358
The earlier disabled state belonged to the old

00:10:38.358 --> 00:10:38.955
controls.

00:10:38.955 --> 00:10:42.070
We'll close this last window after checking its

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.130
initial display.

00:10:47.083 --> 00:10:49.704
You connected an action event to an ordinary

00:10:49.704 --> 00:10:52.146
model, refreshed its view, and tested the

00:10:52.146 --> 00:10:54.171
disabled boundary with real input.

00:10:54.171 --> 00:10:56.851
For another limited resource, what belongs in

00:10:56.851 --> 00:10:59.472
the model's rule, and what feedback must the

00:10:59.472 --> 00:11:00.425
handler refresh?

00:11:00.425 --> 00:11:03.284
Explain how you would check the same action from

00:11:03.284 --> 00:11:04.058
the keyboard.
